HUMAN CHARACTER RULE:
${identity}

RADICAL CHARACTER REPLACEMENT & MORPHOLOGICAL LOCK: Use identity, head design, and EXACT SKELETAL PROPORTIONS from [Character Reference @IMG_1] to REPLACE the subject in [Shot Reference @IMG_2] across ALL 9 FRAMES. @IMG_1 is a turnaround reference: analyze its full 3D body volume and unconventional limb-to-torso ratios. MANDATORY IDENTITY & BODY PURGE: Completely erase all facial features AND the original body type from @IMG_2. @IMG_2 is ONLY a wooden mannequin for pose and lighting. ABSOLUTE ANATOMICAL FIDELITY: Maintain character @IMG_1's unique distorted silhouette, specific curves, and weird anatomical proportions exactly; NO normalization to standard human anatomy. POSE LOCK: Character @IMG_1 MUST adopt the exact body pose from @IMG_2 while keeping its own @IMG_1 skeleton. LIGHTING INTEGRATION: Analyze @IMG_2's environment lighting and project it perfectly onto character @IMG_1. [OUTFIT_INSTRUCTION]
STRICT 9-FRAME 16:9 GRID LAYOUT (3x3):
Frame 1: Extreme Wide Shot (subject tiny in large environment);
Frame 2: Low-Angle Wide Shot (ground level, focusing on full body silhouette);
Frame 3: Classic Medium Shot (Waist-up, looking at camera);
Frame 4: Extreme High-Angle (looking vertically down at top of head);
Frame 5: Profile Shot (Side view, elongated features);
Frame 6: Dutch Angle (horizon tilted 30 degrees, cinematic tension);
Frame 7: Over-the-shoulder shot (looking at a detail in environment);
Frame 8: Three-quarter Medium-Close Up;
Frame 9: Macro ECU (tightest detail on skin texture or eye). MANDATORY: No text on image. No captions, no labels, no watermarks, no overlays. Pure cinematic imagery only.

${videoAttributionBlock('veo31')}

VEO 3.1 / FLOW INGREDIENTS PROMPT - one continuous cinematic shot
Using the provided ingredients, create one continuous photoreal video shot. @image_1 supplies the subject sheet and identity details. @image_2 supplies the final visible look, materials, color grade, and lighting mood. @image_3, if provided, supplies the background/environment ingredient. @video_1 supplies the intended motion path, camera movement, timing, and framing evolution; if the current Flow surface cannot attach video as an ingredient, use @video_1 as the storyboard reference and follow its motion description.

${cam}. The subject from @image_2 / @image_1 performs the action timing from @video_1 with ${prec === 'strict' ? 'very close motion and camera fidelity' : prec === 'balanced' ? 'clean motion fidelity and believable visual continuity' : 'the same motion intention with cinematic interpretation'}. ${videoActionLine(act)}

Style and ambiance: photoreal in-camera footage, final color and light mood from @image_2, optional environment continuity from @image_3, physically plausible movement, stable identity throughout. ${videoPhysLine(phys)}

The shot contains no text overlays, no subtitles unless requested, no watermark, no extra subjects, and no visible artifacts from @video_1's original render style.

